{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>Pinchas ben Elazar, etc.</b> One must know why it was necessary to give his lineage, and perhaps the intention is that his ancestors would be remembered for good. Further, it appears that the intention was to reconcile Israel with Aharon, for he caused a great falling of Israel through the medium of the calf, as it is written (Exodus 32:35): ״וַיִּגֹּף HaShem אֶת הָעָם עַל אֲשֶׁר עָשׂוּ אֶת הָעֵגֶל אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה אַהֲרֹן״. Behold, Pinchas his son's son arose and ransomed the soul of all Israel, as He says immediately afterward: ״וְלֹא כִלִּיתִי אֶת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״. Therefore He traces his lineage back to Aharon. And you will find that they, of blessed memory, said in the Midrash (Tana Devei Eliyahu Rabbah, chapter 13) that Aharon would stand and rectify, and would teach all Israel Torah and good deeds, etc. — end quote. From this you have learned that the corruption that caused the death of a great multitude was reckoned against Aharon, and he would seek to rectify what had been breached; and here HaShem hinted that Pinchas repaid with an abundant good measure." "<b>He turned back My wrath.</b> The intention of informing of this thing is perhaps to say to him that he should not think that HaShem turned back His wrath without the mediation of a merit that overpowered the attribute of justice — and from this Moses might conclude that HaShem conducts Himself in such a manner. Therefore HaShem informed him that Pinchas was the one who was the cause of turning back the wrath, as it says <b>He turned back My wrath</b> — and it did not turn back of itself." "<b>From upon the children of Israel.</b> Meaning: after the wrath had gone forth and was upon them, he turned it back from upon them — and this is a great praise. Further, the intention is to speak of the good deed in terms of the action itself: that he removed the anger of HaShem, for anger is a branch of sorrow and grief, and it yields much nachat ruach (satisfaction) to HaShem when wrath is turned back." "<b>When he was zealous with My zeal among them.</b> He was precise in saying <b>My zeal</b>, and also said <b>among them</b>. He comes to give power to the one who grasps through it the turning back of wrath, and He says that it came about through the mediation of three things. The first: that he was zealous for His honor and Himself and risked himself, as stated in their words, and this is what it says <b>when he was zealous</b>, in the construct form. Second: that the zeal was for My honor alone, not for the sake of any intermediary thing besides it — and this is a complete mitzvah in which HaShem takes more pleasure than in anything else a person may do, and this is what it says <b>My zeal</b>, in the construct form referring to the Blessed One. Third: that the action he performed was not hidden from human eyes, but he sanctified HaShem within a congregation and assembly, as they of blessed memory said (Sanhedrin 82b) that all the tribe of Shimon were gathered around the tent where he pierced both of them and killed their prince before their eyes, and this is what it says <b>among them</b>." "<b>And I did not consume, etc.</b> One needs to know why He wrote the cause — which is ״when he was zealous with My zeal among them״ — in between the two details of what resulted, which are ״He turned back My wrath״ and ״I did not consume, etc.״, for He should have said it in this manner: ״when he was zealous, etc., He turned back, etc., and I did not consume״, etc., or in this manner: ״He turned back, etc., and I did not consume, etc., when he was zealous״, etc.?" "However, it is intended to note that the turning back of the wrath after it had been upon the children of Israel — this stage was attained only by means of the three supreme gradations recorded in the matter, as I explained in the verse ״בְּקַנְאוֹ״ etc. But regarding the prevention of the destruction, where He said ״וְלֹא כִלִּיתִי,״ it is possible that HaShem would also leave a remnant for His people even if the deed did not possess all three aforementioned levels, and through the prayer of a righteous man and the like, by virtue of the merit, HaShem would say to the destroying angel ״רַב״ etc. Also, according to what I explained in His saying ״הֵשִׁיב אֶת חֲמָתִי״ — that it is a good thing in the category of action in and of itself — the verse must of necessity speak in this order. For if it had said ״הֵשִׁיב״ etc. ״וְלֹא כִלִּיתִי״ and then afterward said ״בְּקַנְאוֹ,״ it would have been heard as though the ultimate purpose of this matter of turning back the wrath is that which follows from it — that He did not consume the children of Israel — and it would not be understood that there is a good effect in the turning back of wrath in and of itself. But from His saying ״הֵשִׁיב אֶת חֲמָתִי״ and His stating immediately adjacent to it the cause of the matter, which is ״בְּקַנְאוֹ״ etc., this tells that He had already indicated the ultimate purpose of the good effect."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:14:33.597413857Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}